WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.086 --> 00:00:05.709
Everyone in Prickly Pear was excited about that gold.

2
00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.857
But Sheriff Roscoe was just thinking about his job.

3
00:00:09.858 --> 00:00:14.277
He turned to Cleo. "Can you send a telegram to the newspapers?

4
00:00:14.278 --> 00:00:16.479
They can print a notice about the gold.

5
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.942
I'll go and lock up the sack."

6
00:00:18.943 --> 00:00:23.958
"Sure," Cleo said.

7
00:00:23.959 --> 00:00:29.679
In the sheriff's office, Roscoe put the sack inside the safe.

8
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.781
"I'll set a new code for the combination lock," he thought.

9
00:00:33.782 --> 00:00:36.339
"I'll use numbers that I won't forget."

10
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.821
He carefully turned the dial to different numbers.

11
00:00:40.822 --> 00:00:48.712
"Thirty-six, five, seventeen." Roscoe shut the safe. Click!

12
00:00:48.713 --> 00:00:52.299
He went outside.

13
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.839
Cleo was helping a farmer remove a wheel from his wagon.

14
00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.603
The wheel was broken. It

15
00:00:58.604 --> 00:01:01.699
was also stuck. The

16
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:07.359
farmer hit the wheel with a hammer to loosen it. Roscoe

17
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.988
and Cleo pulled. Finally

18
00:01:10.989 --> 00:01:14.859
the wheel popped off. "Thanks!"

19
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.742
the farmer said. "I

20
00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:21.297
hope the repair shop has a 40-inch wheel." Cleo

21
00:01:21.298 --> 00:01:24.050
turned to Roscoe. "Alright,

22
00:01:24.051 --> 00:01:26.467
Sheriff. Let's send that—" "Oh

23
00:01:26.468 --> 00:01:31.218
no!" someone shouted. "That

24
00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:34.279
came from the stable!" Roscoe said. He

25
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:38.041
and Cleo ran to the stable. Jake,

26
00:01:38.042 --> 00:01:40.999
the stable owner, was upset. "I

27
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.053
just got back from breakfast. Two

28
00:01:44.054 --> 00:01:48.276
horses are missing!" Roscoe

29
00:01:48.277 --> 00:01:56.107
and Cleo searched everywhere. But

30
00:01:56.108 --> 00:02:02.732
the dirt was covered with too many hoofprints. After

31
00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:07.053
a couple of hours, they gave up. Roscoe

32
00:02:07.054 --> 00:02:13.219
sighed. "Maybe the horses will come back on their own. Let's

33
00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:19.925
send that telegram now." ***

34
00:02:19.926 --> 00:02:23.353
Fritzy sat down at his telegraph machine.

35
00:02:23.354 --> 00:02:27.059
"What should the telegram say, Sheriff?"

36
00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:31.449
"'A sack was found in Prickly Pear,'" Roscoe said.

37
00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.197
"'Its contents are valuable.'"

38
00:02:34.198 --> 00:02:39.319
Fritzy tapped on the telegraph machine, but nothing happened.

39
00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.234
"Hmm," he said.

40
00:02:42.235 --> 00:02:49.604
"The machine isn't working. We need to check the line."

41
00:02:49.605 --> 00:02:54.042
*** They followed the telegraph line outside of

42
00:02:54.043 --> 00:02:59.139
town. Roscoe and Cleo rode alongside Fritzy's

43
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:05.179
wagon. Around noon they spotted a wire hanging down from a

44
00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:08.480
pole. Everyone

45
00:03:08.481 --> 00:03:12.579
stopped. "Some of the wires are old," Fritzy

46
00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.612
said. "They wear out and then

47
00:03:16.613 --> 00:03:20.395
break." He ran around to a spool of new wire.

48
00:03:20.396 --> 00:03:24.088
"We'll need 16 feet of wire."

49
00:03:24.089 --> 00:03:28.665
Roscoe walked backward, unwinding the wire.

50
00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:32.236
Cleo watched as the wire came off the spool.

51
00:03:32.237 --> 00:03:37.339
"Three feet . . . four feet . . . five feet . . ."

52
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.959
Fritzy unloaded his toolbox.

53
00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.731
"Fifteen . . . ," Cleo said as the wire came off the spool.

54
00:03:45.732 --> 00:03:50.473
"And . . . 16 feet! Stop!"

55
00:03:50.474 --> 00:03:55.909
Fritzy gasped. He was looking at the old wire.

56
00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.959
"This wire isn't worn out."

57
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.865
Cleo scratched her head. "What does that mean?"

58
00:04:03.866 --> 00:04:11.390
Fritzy frowned. "It means someone cut it to stop us from sending that telegram."

59
00:04:11.391 --> 00:04:21.473
Roscoe nodded. "And it could be anyone in town!"

